# Core Architecture & System Design (SOLID)
- Enforce the Single Responsibility Principle: Components should do one thing. Extract complex logic into custom hooks, utility functions, or Server Actions.
- Favor composition over inheritance. Use `children` props and layout patterns to avoid deep prop drilling.
- Maintain strict separation of concerns: Server Components for data fetching/SEO, Client Components solely for interactivity (onClick, state, browser APIs).
- Keep file sizes small. Extract interfaces and types to separate files if they exceed 30 lines.

# Next.js 16 & React App Router
- Default to Server Components (`page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`). Add `"use client"` only when strictly necessary for interactivity or Redux hooks.
- Use Next.js Server Actions for all data mutations. Implement `useActionState` (React 19) for form states and `useFormStatus` for pending UI.
- Fetch data natively using `fetch` or direct ORM/DB calls inside Server Components. Do not use `useEffect` for initial data fetching.
- Utilize Partial Prerendering (PPR) patterns by wrapping dynamic server components in `<Suspense>` boundaries.
- Adhere to the latest Next.js 16 caching defaults (queries are uncached by default). Explicitly define `cache: 'force-cache'` or `next: { revalidate: X }` where static generation is required.

# Styling (Tailwind CSS & shadcn/ui)
- Use standard Tailwind utility classes. Avoid arbitrary values (e.g., `h-[42px]`) unless absolutely necessary; use the design system scales.
- Utilize the `cn()` utility (clsx + tailwind-merge) for conditional classes and merging custom component props safely.
- Import and use `shadcn/ui` components from `@/components/ui/` for all core UI elements (Buttons, Dialogs, Forms) before building custom equivalents.

# State Management (Redux Toolkit)
- Treat Redux as a purely Client-Side state manager for complex, highly interactive global UI state (e.g., audio players, complex multi-step wizards, websocket streams). 
- Do NOT use Redux to store server data. Server data belongs in Server Components, React Server cache, or URL search params.
- Use strictly typed hooks: `useAppDispatch` and `useAppSelector`. Never use raw `useDispatch` or `useSelector`.
- Group Redux logic using the "feature folder" pattern (e.g., `features/cart/cartSlice.ts`).

# SEO & Metadata (OGP & Geo)
- Every `page.tsx` and `layout.tsx` must export a strongly typed `generateMetadata` function or a static `metadata` object.
- Always include `title`, `description`, `openGraph` (title, description, url, siteName, images), and `twitter` card metadata.
- For local or regional pages, strictly implement GEO tags in the metadata block (e.g., `alternates`, `robots`, and custom region tags).

# Accessibility (ARIA, WCAG & Semantic HTML)
- Always use semantic HTML5 elements (`<article>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`). Avoid `<div>` soup.
- Ensure all interactive elements have appropriate `aria-label`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, or `aria-hidden` attributes where visual context isn't programmatically available.
- Form inputs must have explicitly associated `<label>` elements (using `htmlFor`).
- Maintain strict keyboard navigability. Ensure all interactive elements are focusable and have visible focus states using Tailwind's `focus-visible:ring`.
- Enforce WCAG AA color contrast ratios for all text and background combinations.